A||||6||jlhuber||couple  questions here ||||5||
Z||000000||jlhuber||02-13-2000||05:12 PM||||&gt;it has been said that an easy way to get the 2.8 into the #4 or #1 firing position, you line up the timing marks on the crank.  does it matter that i find mulitple marks on my crank?  by crank i am meaning harmonic balancer.  the only difference in them is that one is just slightly bigger than the others.  this is the mark that i should be using, right?<BR>&gt;what chips are you guys and gals running?  in the fiero store catalog they offer the ADS auto-street chip & auto-strip chip and the HYPERTECH auto-street runner & auto-thermomaster.  i have the stock 2.8 matted to the stock 3spd auto transaxle.  what would you reccomend for my application.  i am not running on the strip so i can prob. mark the ADS strip chip out.  i like to street race when i get a safe chance to.  i guess the best way to put it is i want the best chip for everyday driving and quite a bit of street racing.<BR>&gt;a while ago someone mentioned an oil filter that was the same dia/thread as the stock 2.8 filter, only longer.  anyone remember what it was?]<BR>&gt;any help is appreciated. TIA<BR>                       -Jess||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phaeton||02-13-2000||05:42 PM||||Oil filter should be pf52 if pf47 is stock, or the other way around? anyway they both fit and one is bigger than the other.  The balancer should have a large obvious line across it, if not, then it's the old finger in the sparkplug hole trick.  But if the slightly larger mark resembles a bite made by a very small animal,THAT'S IT.  If it's just a larger scratch then who knows.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mwbackus||02-13-2000||09:04 PM||||Most aftermarket chips are a waste of money.  The Hypertech Thermomaster may be a consideration if you really want the benefits of extremely low (160°) operating temperatures, but you also need a 160° stat and the #4026 fan switch.  I suggest switching to a 180° stat and using the Hypertech 185° #4028 fan switch (avail. from summit). It works fine with the stock chip and then you can spend the money you saved on a high output coil and a mult-spark ignition box.  You will be much happier with the results.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||02-13-2000||10:43 PM||||The filter to use is pf51 I don't even know if they make the pf47 anymore. I do know that NAPA auto parts says to use pf51. (it might be pf52 I don't remember the exact number) ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rodney||02-13-2000||11:01 PM||||If you want to go to the 180 degree Stant thermostat (Part # 13818) I sell a 185-200 degree fan switch. The 185 degree Hypertech switch from Summit is $42.95. My 185-200 fan switch is $28.50. I also stock the Stant 13818 thermostat for $7.00. <BR> <A HREF="http://www.execpc.com/~rodney1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.execpc.com/~rodney1/</A> <P>Rodney Dickman||||reg||1
Z||000005||mwbackus||02-14-2000||11:09 AM||||Note:  I also suggest buying the items I referred to above from Rodney!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierosound||02-14-2000||12:40 PM||||Performance ECM chips are available from 5 vendors.  These are ADS, Hypertech, FastChip, Performance Resources and SuperChip<P>Check the thread below.  I got the impression most people at this forum are running with stock chips.<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001709.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001709.html</A> <P><p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 02-14-2000).]||||reg||10
